// Probes for category: logic
// Atoms: 6 — constraint_satisfaction, conditional_rule_application, set_operations, deductive_chain, boolean_eval, composite_truth_judgment
// Probes per atom: 15 (5 × L1 + 5 × L2 + 5 × L3) — total 90.
// Scorer types used: exact_match_string, regex.
// Notes:
//   - All constraint_satisfaction puzzles have been hand-verified to admit exactly one solution.
//   - Boolean expressions follow standard Python-style precedence: NOT > AND > OR.
//   - For list-answer probes, regex scorers tolerate flexible whitespace around commas.
//   - "cannot determine" is included as an option in deductive_chain probes to avoid forcing yes/no.
// Subagent review (Explore) findings and resolutions:
//   - FIXED: logic.deductive_chain.L2.004 — premise 3 was a necessary condition; rewrote as sufficient ("Anyone who qualifies for license T may operate type-Z aircraft") so the chain Pat→200hr→T→Z gives "yes" deductively.
//   - FIXED: logic.composite_truth_judgment.L3.{002,003,004} — diversified answer keys (was all TF); now L3 covers TF, TT, FF, FT, TF.
//   - FIXED: logic.constraint_satisfaction.L3.005 — added "i" flag to regex for consistency with all other CSP scorers.
//   - FIXED: logic.constraint_satisfaction.L1.{004,005} — replaced near-duplicate "3 categorical values + 1 negation + 1 equality" puzzles with structurally different ones (numeric ordering puzzle for L1.004; 4-card non-equality puzzle for L1.005).
//   - DECLINED (intentional): logic.conditional_rule_application.L1.{003,005} use closed-world / iff reading of policy rules. This matches the atom card framing where rules are interpreted as biconditional permissions in the contexts examined; the prompt language ("a user may ... if X" treated as iff for permission-grant rules) is standard for this atom.
//   - DECLINED (intentional): logic.composite_truth_judgment scorer is case-sensitive on TT/TF/FT/FF. The prompt explicitly requires capital letters; this is a structured-label test, not a free-form answer.
//   - DECLINED (informational): logic.composite_truth_judgment leans on factual recall, but per the atom card definition this is the intended verb ("given several truth-evaluable claims, produce a single composite output"). The atom is about combining verdicts, not deriving them.
