{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L2.004","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"thematically_linked"},"prompt":"Both statements concern the classification of chemical elements. Evaluate each as true or false.\n\nStatement 1: Iron is classified as a transition metal.\nStatement 2: Calcium is classified as a transition metal.\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"TF","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"TF","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["composite","chemistry"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L2.005","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"thematically_linked"},"prompt":"Both statements concern the U.S. Constitution. Evaluate each as true or false.\n\nStatement 1: The Bill of Rights consists of the first ten amendments.\nStatement 2: The Twenty-Fifth Amendment establishes procedures for presidential succession and the handling of presidential disability.\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"TT","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"TT","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["composite","civics"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L3.001","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"logically_coupled"},"prompt":"Evaluate each statement as true or false. Evaluate their truth values separately; do not assume that a statement and its converse have the same truth value.\n\nStatement 1: Every prime number greater than 2 is odd.\nStatement 2: Every odd integer greater than 2 is prime.\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"TF","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"TF","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["composite","converse"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L3.002","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"independent_definition_checks"},"prompt":"Evaluate each statement as true or false. Note: judge each statement on its own merits.\n\nStatement 1: In any regular polygon, all sides are congruent (equal in length).\nStatement 2: In any regular polygon, all interior angles are congruent (equal in measure).\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"TT","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"TT","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["composite","independent_truths","geometry"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L3.003","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"logically_coupled"},"prompt":"Evaluate each statement as true or false.\n\nStatement 1: Every prime number is odd.\nStatement 2: Every odd integer is prime.\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"FF","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"FF","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["composite","coupled_falsehoods","number_theory"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L3.004","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"logically_coupled"},"prompt":"In standard Euclidean geometry, using the usual definitions, evaluate each statement as true or false. Judge each statement independently.\n\nStatement 1: Every parallelogram is a rhombus.\nStatement 2: Every rhombus is a parallelogram.\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"FT","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"FT","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["composite","converse","geometry"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment.L3.005","canonical_id":"logic.composite_truth_judgment","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_statements":"two","claim_independence":"logically_coupled"},"prompt":"Evaluate each statement as true or false. Note: judge each statement on its own merits.\n\nStatement 1: If a quadrilateral is a rhombus, then its diagonals are perpendicular.\nStatement 2: If a quadrilateral has perpendicular diagonals, then it is a rhombus.\n\nOutput exactly one of: TT, TF, FT, FF.","ground_truth":"TF","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"TF","case_sensitive":true,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["composite","converse","geometry"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L1.001","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_variables":"≤4","constraint_type":"mixed_equality_and_non_equality"},"prompt":"Three friends — Alice, Bob, and Carol — each chose a different color from {red, blue, green}.\n- Alice did not choose red.\n- Bob chose blue.\n\nWhat color did Carol choose? Reply with exactly one of: red / blue / green","ground_truth":"red","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"red","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["csp","minimal"],"notes":"Bob=blue; Alice not red so Alice=green; Carol=red."}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L1.002","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_variables":"≤4","constraint_type":"ordering"},"prompt":"Three books — Math, History, and Science — are placed in a row of three positions (left to right: position 1, 2, 3). Each position holds exactly one book.\n- The Math book is not in position 1.\n- The Science book is to the right of the Math book.\n\nWhat is the order from position 1 to position 3? Output the three book names separated by ', ' with single spaces after commas. Example format: 'A, B, C'.","ground_truth":"History, Math, Science","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*History\\s*,\\s*Math\\s*,\\s*Science\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","ordering"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L1.003","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_variables":"≤4","constraint_type":"ordering"},"prompt":"Four runners — P, Q, R, S — finished a race in some order (1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th place). Each runner has a distinct finishing position.\n- P finished before Q.\n- R finished last (4th).\n- S finished first (1st).\n\nOutput the finishing order from 1st place to 4th place as four letters separated by ', '. Example format: 'A, B, C, D'.","ground_truth":"S, P, Q, R","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*S\\s*,\\s*P\\s*,\\s*Q\\s*,\\s*R\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","ordering"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L1.004","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_variables":"≤4","constraint_type":"ordering"},"prompt":"Three numbers — labelled x, y, z — take the values 5, 7, and 9 (each value used exactly once).\n- x is the largest of the three.\n- y is less than z.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'x=N, y=N, z=N'. Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"x=9, y=5, z=7","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*x=9, y=5, z=7\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","ordering"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L1.005","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_variables":"≤4","constraint_type":"non-equality"},"prompt":"Four cards lie face-down in a row, in positions 1, 2, 3, 4 (left to right). Each card is a different suit drawn from {hearts, spades, clubs, diamonds}.\n- The card in position 1 is hearts.\n- The card in position 4 is diamonds.\n- The card in position 2 is not spades.\n\nWhat suit is the card in position 3? Reply with exactly one of: hearts / spades / clubs / diamonds","ground_truth":"spades","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"spades","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["csp","minimal"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L2.001","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_variables":"5–10","constraint_type":"ordering"},"prompt":"Five vehicles — sedan, truck, van, coupe, suv — are parked in spots numbered 1 to 5 (left to right). Each spot holds exactly one vehicle.\n- The sedan is in a spot to the left of the truck.\n- The coupe is in spot 3.\n- The van is in spot 1.\n- The suv is in a spot to the right of the truck.\n\nOutput the order from spot 1 to spot 5 as vehicle names separated by ', '. Example format: 'a, b, c, d, e'.","ground_truth":"van, sedan, coupe, truck, suv","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*van\\s*,\\s*sedan\\s*,\\s*coupe\\s*,\\s*truck\\s*,\\s*suv\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","backtracking"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L2.002","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_variables":"4","constraint_type":"ordering"},"prompt":"Four employees — P, Q, R, S — are each scheduled for a shift on a different day from {Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday}.\n- P works on Monday or Thursday.\n- Q works the day immediately before R.\n- S works on Tuesday.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'P=Day, Q=Day, R=Day, S=Day' using full day names (e.g. Monday). Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"P=Monday, Q=Wednesday, R=Thursday, S=Tuesday","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*P\\s*=\\s*Monday, Q\\s*=\\s*Wednesday, R\\s*=\\s*Thursday, S\\s*=\\s*Tuesday\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","backtracking"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L2.003","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_variables":"4","constraint_type":"mixed_position_and_adjacency"},"prompt":"Four houses on a street are numbered 1 to 4 from left to right. Each house is painted a different color from {red, blue, green, yellow}.\n- The red house is not at either end of the street.\n- The blue house is immediately to the left of the green house.\n- House 4 is yellow.\n\nOutput the colors from house 1 to house 4 as four color names separated by ', '. Example format: 'a, b, c, d'.","ground_truth":"blue, green, red, yellow","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*blue\\s*,\\s*green\\s*,\\s*red\\s*,\\s*yellow\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","backtracking"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L2.004","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_variables":"5–10","constraint_type":"non-equality"},"prompt":"Five children — Eve, Finn, Gus, Hana, Ira — sit in a row of seats numbered 1 to 5 (left to right). Each seat holds exactly one child.\n- Eve sits in seat 1.\n- Ira sits in seat 2.\n- Finn sits in a seat immediately next to Hana.\n- Gus sits in seat 3 or seat 5.\n- Hana sits in an even-numbered seat.\n- Finn sits in a seat to the left of Gus.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'seat1=Name, seat2=Name, seat3=Name, seat4=Name, seat5=Name'. Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"seat1=Eve, seat2=Ira, seat3=Finn, seat4=Hana, seat5=Gus","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*seat1=Eve, seat2=Ira, seat3=Finn, seat4=Hana, seat5=Gus\\s*$","flags":[]},"tags":["csp","backtracking"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L2.005","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_variables":"4","constraint_type":"ordering"},"prompt":"Four professors — Lin, Mei, Noor, Owen — each teach a class in a different time slot from {9am, 10am, 11am, 12pm}.\n- Lin teaches at an earlier time than Mei.\n- Noor teaches at 11am.\n- Owen does not teach at 9am.\n- Mei does not teach at 10am.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'Lin=time, Mei=time, Noor=time, Owen=time' using exactly the time labels above (e.g. 9am). Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"Lin=9am, Mei=12pm, Noor=11am, Owen=10am","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^Lin=9am, Mei=12pm, Noor=11am, Owen=10am$","flags":[]},"tags":["csp","backtracking"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L3.001","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_variables":"5–10","constraint_type":"disjunction"},"prompt":"Five guests — A, B, C, D, E — sit in a row of chairs numbered 1 to 5 (left to right). Each chair holds exactly one guest.\n- A is not adjacent to B (their chair numbers differ by more than 1).\n- C sits in chair 1 or chair 5.\n- D's chair number is strictly between A's and B's chair numbers.\n- E sits in chair 3.\n- A sits in an even-numbered chair.\n- B sits in a chair to the right of A.\n\nOutput the assignments in the order chair1 through chair5, using the form 'chair1=X, chair2=X, chair3=X, chair4=X, chair5=X'. Whitespace around punctuation is allowed.","ground_truth":"chair1=C, chair2=A, chair3=E, chair4=D, chair5=B","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*chair1\\s*=\\s*C\\s*,\\s*chair2\\s*=\\s*A\\s*,\\s*chair3\\s*=\\s*E\\s*,\\s*chair4\\s*=\\s*D\\s*,\\s*chair5\\s*=\\s*B\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","disjunction"],"notes":"Verified unique: A=2 forces B=5,D=4,C=1; A=4 has no valid B/D."}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L3.002","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_variables":"4","constraint_type":"mixed_ordering_fixed_and_nonadjacent"},"prompt":"Four workshops — W1, W2, W3, W4 — are scheduled in four time slots — S1, S2, S3, S4 (in chronological order). Each slot holds exactly one workshop.\n- W1 is held in an earlier slot than W3.\n- W2 is held in S2 or S4.\n- W4 is NOT held in the slot immediately after W1.\n- W3 is held in S3.\n- W4 is held in a slot later than W2.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'W1=Si, W2=Si, W3=Si, W4=Si'. Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"W1=S1, W2=S2, W3=S3, W4=S4","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^W1=S1, W2=S2, W3=S3, W4=S4$","flags":[]},"tags":["csp","disjunction"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L3.003","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_variables":"5–10","constraint_type":"disjunction"},"prompt":"In a row of 5 lockers (numbered 1 to 5), five students — P, Q, R, S, T — each have one locker. No two students share a locker.\n- P's locker number is odd.\n- R is in locker 4.\n- Q's locker number is strictly between P's and S's locker numbers.\n- T is NOT adjacent to R (T's locker number is not 3 and not 5).\n- S is in locker 1 or locker 5.\n- P's locker number is greater than S's locker number.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'locker1=X, locker2=X, locker3=X, locker4=X, locker5=X'. Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"locker1=S, locker2=T, locker3=Q, locker4=R, locker5=P","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*locker1=S, locker2=T, locker3=Q, locker4=R, locker5=P\\s*$","flags":[]},"tags":["csp","disjunction"],"notes":"Verified unique: only S=1,P=5 case satisfies all constraints."}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L3.004","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_variables":"5–10","constraint_type":"disjunction"},"prompt":"Five students — S1, S2, S3, S4, S5 — each enroll in a different subject from {Math, Eng, Bio, Chem, Hist}.\n- S1 enrolls in Math or Bio.\n- S2 enrolls in Hist.\n- S3 does not enroll in Eng.\n- S3 enrolls in Math or Chem.\n- S4 enrolls in Math or Eng.\n- S5's subject begins with the letter C.\n\nOutput the assignments as 'S1=Subject, S2=Subject, S3=Subject, S4=Subject, S5=Subject' using exactly the subject names above. Use single spaces after commas.","ground_truth":"S1=Bio, S2=Hist, S3=Math, S4=Eng, S5=Chem","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^S1=Bio, S2=Hist, S3=Math, S4=Eng, S5=Chem$","flags":[]},"tags":["csp","disjunction"]}
{"probe_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction.L3.005","canonical_id":"logic.constraint_satisfaction","category":"logic","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_variables":"5–10","constraint_type":"disjunction"},"prompt":"Six points — P1, P2, P3, P4, P5, P6 — are placed at distinct integer coordinates chosen from {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. Each point gets exactly one coordinate.\n- P1's coordinate is 2 or 5.\n- P2's coordinate is 3 or 4.\n- P2's coordinate is less than P3's coordinate.\n- P4 is at coordinate 6.\n- |coord(P5) − coord(P3)| = 1.\n- P5's coordinate is greater than P3's coordinate.\n- P6 is at coordinate 1.\n\nOutput the assignments in the order P1, P2, P3, P4, P5, P6, using the form `P1=c, P2=c, P3=c, P4=c, P5=c, P6=c`.","ground_truth":"P1=2, P2=3, P3=4, P4=6, P5=5, P6=1","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*P1\\s*=\\s*2\\s*,\\s*P2\\s*=\\s*3\\s*,\\s*P3\\s*=\\s*4\\s*,\\s*P4\\s*=\\s*6\\s*,\\s*P5\\s*=\\s*5\\s*,\\s*P6\\s*=\\s*1\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["csp","disjunction"]}
